<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.2.1: Different Forms of Perfect Squares</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02350</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.2.1: Different Forms of Perfect Squares</md:title>
    <md:uuid>f0caf220-9903-433b-a03a-8220d062a33a</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section>
    <section class="check-understanding">
      <title>Warm Up Activity</title>
      <exercise data-content-id="48f644b8-f3c3-4365-8e2f-aad6c1ca3ec3">
        <problem>
          <para>
      <para><emphasis>Select three</emphasis> expressions that are perfect squares. </para>
      </para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>["\\((x+5)(5+x)\\)", "\\((x−3)^2\\)", "\\(x^2+8x+16\\)"]</para>
          <para>
      <para>The correct answers are: \((x+5)(5+x)\), \((x−3)^2\), \(x^2+8x+16\).</para>
    </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
    <example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Explain how you know the expressions you selected are perfect squares.</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>Because each of them is the product of an expression and itself. \(x^2+8x+16\) is equivalent to \((x+4)(x+4)\).</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
  </section>
</content>
